course: dictionary
lesson: 16080
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them tell you which one is meant. «Mum wants to choose new wallpaper for my bedroom.» Here wallpaper means paper with patterns that covers the walls of a room.
explain: «Her phone wallpaper is a photo of her puppy.» The clues are phone and photo. Wallpaper here is the background picture behind the icons on a screen.
explain: «We wallpapered the hall on Saturday.» Wallpapered is a doing word, so it is a verb. It means put wallpaper on the walls. Check by swapping in your meaning: «We put paper on the walls on Saturday» still makes sense.
fact: Wallpaper is a noun for paper with patterns or colours that decorates walls.
fact: On a phone or computer, wallpaper is the background picture behind your apps and icons.
fact: To wallpaper means to put wallpaper on walls: «They wallpapered the kitchen in yellow stripes.»
fact: When you mean the material in general, you say some wallpaper, not a wallpaper.
exercise: «The bedroom wallpaper has little green stars on it.» What is wallpaper here? | A picture on a phone screen | A paint for a garden fence | *Paper with patterns that covers a wall | A kind of bedroom lamp
exercise: «Ella changed her phone wallpaper to a photo of the beach.» What does wallpaper mean here? | *The background picture on the screen | A game you play on the phone | A cover for a school book | A paper for the walls
exercise: «On Saturday we wallpapered the hall.» What does wallpapered mean? | Cleaned the windows | *Covered the walls with wallpaper | Painted with a brush | Took up the carpet
test: «Dad wants to put up new wallpaper in the lounge.» What does put up wallpaper mean? | To hang pictures on a string | To buy a new carpet | *To stick wallpaper onto the walls | To tear down an old wall
test: Sam says his phone wallpaper is a dinosaur. Where can you see the wallpaper? | *On the background of the phone screen | Inside the fridge | In a bedroom cupboard | On a bus timetable
exam: Which sentence uses wallpaper as a verb? | «The wallpaper is peeling off the wall.» | *«We are wallpapering the hall this weekend.» | «My phone wallpaper is a sunset.» | «I like the blue wallpaper in my room.»
